WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.280
SALLY HOROWITZ: My name is
Sally Finkelstein Horowitz

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.140
and I come from Poland.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:23.380
I was born in
Zwolen, A small town.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:26.740
And when the war broke
out, I was 11 years old.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:32.590
And on September 3rd,
the bombs came to Zwolen,

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:36.520
even though it was not
a industrialized city.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.670
And then when the
bombers flew away,

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:43.050
the place was a
complete like a inferno,

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.180
complete and engulfed in fire.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.360
And I was scared, because I
couldn't get to my parents.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.190
And then after this, the
Germans, the planes came again.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:57.910
And they were spraying the
people with machine guns.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.600
And then we were in
the woods overnight.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.720
We survived that.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:06.250
And then when we came back
home, everything was bombed out.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.280
And we just huddled
together in little rooms

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.200
with whoever we could.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.240
And then, there was the ghetto.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.930
We were all carted to
one place of the city.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.570
And it wasn't a big
town, but they still

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:22.420
brought in from different
small towns people.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.850
And everybody was
in the same place,

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.085
sleeping wherever they could.

00:01:27.085 --> 00:01:28.210
It was terrible.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.930
Then they were patrolling
us all the time.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.380
And sometimes the Germans would
knock at the doors at night,

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:38.380
and say, "Ich bin ein
Arzt," I'm a doctor.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.150
And if there were girls, they
said they have to inspect them.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.360
They weren't doctors.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.820
So they made a little
hole in the wall,

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.250
so we could run out and hide.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.620
And there was also a
synagogue in our town,

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.800
which I cannot forget,
because it was--

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.840
the paintings were
on the ceiling,

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.670
and there were very thick walls,
and the doors were very thick.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:02.950
And I was always in
awe of that synagogue.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:06.890
I always felt like it's
a very holy, holy place.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.560
Well, this was burned down.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.270
And when we went in the ghetto,
my father and my little brother

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:16.620
went out to hide in a village.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.600
And I somehow refused
to be in the ghetto.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.940
I just always wanted to get out.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.160
So one time I went
to visit my father.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.470
I was very, very
close to my father.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.080
And all of a sudden, I
was sitting, and I feel--

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.110
I see somebody standing
there, so I picked up my head,

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.650
and there was a
man standing there.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.380
And he says to me, "Who are you?

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.550
Who did you come to visit?"

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.700
Because they knew who was
supposed to be in the villages.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.060
And this was a Polish man.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.148
He was the eldest
of the village.

00:02:45.148 --> 00:02:47.190
And I told him I came to
visit the Jewish people.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.260
So he started to yell,
"Are you Jewish?"

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.130
And I say, "Yes."

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.008
He says "You wait right here."

00:02:52.008 --> 00:02:53.550
He's going to bring
me to the Germans

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.870
for coming out of the ghetto.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.060
And I ran across the street.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.070
I became friendly with
one of the girls there,

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.690
the Polish girls.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.200
And I told her what happened.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.900
And I told her, "I'm scared."

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.620
So she said, "Come with me,"
and we went up in the attic

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.060
to hide in the attic.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.155
I remember my heart
was pounding so bad.

00:03:11.155 --> 00:03:12.030
And she was laughing.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.390
She says, "Don't be scared."

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:14.010
I was scared.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.960
I was the Jew.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.030
So he kept asking everybody,
"Where's that Jew?

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.390
Did anybody see that Jew?

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.200
Where is the Jew?"

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.743
But nobody told him anything.

00:03:22.743 --> 00:03:23.910
So they went to look for me.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.160
Of course, that's when he
started to scream and holler

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.300
where I was.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.770
And then when he
went away, I started

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.020
to run and look for my father.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.160
And I was scared.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.190
My little brother was, at the
time, about six years old.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.020
And so my father
says, "Don't worry.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.015
I will give him
a bottle of vodka

00:03:39.015 --> 00:03:40.140
so he wouldn't bother you."

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.360
But meanwhile, I was scared.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.110
Now, we were in the
ghetto for a while.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:43.652
I don't know.

00:03:43.652 --> 00:03:45.570
It must have been a year or so.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.550
And it was horrible.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:51.300
They were always
taking us to work.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:56.100
And they took out the
rabbi, and they make him--

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.530
they made him walk on,
uh, the Sefer Torah.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.606
They would pull his beard.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:17.860
They were pulling his beard
and blood was coming out.

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:23.694
And, uh, nobody helped him.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.047
And then every other day,
there was something else.

00:04:32.047 --> 00:04:34.380
We were sleeping and all of
a sudden we heard the church

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:40.052
bells, and trumpets, and bands,
whatever they did, "Juden,

00:04:40.052 --> 00:04:42.080
heraus, alle Juden heraus."

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.490
And we all had to get out
and meet in the market place.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:51.540
And there was a lot of
shooting and a lot of hitting.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.360
And then they made us
march 12 kilometers.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.860
And for some reason,
a certain person,

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.830
the people we stayed
with in the ghetto times,

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.580
one of their
daughters got married.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:07.920
She was 17 or 18.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.530
And she was pregnant.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:15.900
And we were walking the 12
kilometers, and she was--

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.140
until I had my own children, I
never thought of her too much,

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.360
but I can't forget her.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.680
She was very big.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.720
She must have been in the
eighth or ninth month.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:25.770
And it was very warm.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:33.215
It was the second
days of in September,

00:05:33.215 --> 00:05:37.220
it was a holiday, Sukkot,
the second day of the Sukkot.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:39.320
And she wore her
father's trench coat.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.180
And her feet were very swollen.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:43.640
And my mother had,
um, some water.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:46.300
She always used to give
out a little bit of water.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:50.090
And then we came to
the train station

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:53.750
because Zwolen did not have
a train station at the time.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:55.790
And we all sat
down on the ground.

00:05:55.790 --> 00:06:01.000
And we were joined by people
from different smaller cities

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.870
and ghettos.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:04.772
And there were
trains in back of us,

00:06:04.772 --> 00:06:05.980
we all sitting on the ground.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:08.910
There must have been
10,000 people or so.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:12.170
And we could smell like
chlorine from the trains.

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:16.650
But we didn't know this
was going to be for us.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:22.580
And while we were there, one of
my sisters, a few days before

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:26.290
was taken to a farm, which was
a few kilometers from my home

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:27.240
town.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.470
And I don't know, for some
reason, one of the Germans

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:34.820
was walking by where I was
sitting, and he pointed to me.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:36.950
And I said-- no, he was looking.

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:37.850
And I got scared.

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:42.740
And my mother made me bend down
and hide myself behind her,

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:44.780
because we didn't
know what will happen.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:47.420
So he passed by
again, and he pointed

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.710
I should get up,
and come, "raus."

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:53.570
And my mother pushed
my other sister.

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.280
This was her fear, "go, you'll
have to take care of her.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:57.780
She won't be able
to wash her hair."

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.270
This is the last
words I remember.

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:06.420
And they took-- he took
out about 21 women, girls.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:08.350
And then everybody
started to run.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:11.170
Everybody sensed
something wrong.

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:13.210
And there was a lot
of shooting, and a lot

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:15.640
of beatings and the shootings.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:19.250
And we were walked another
three miles to the farm

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:23.320
where my sister was there.

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:25.810
And we worked there
on the potato farm.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.570
And then when it got cold
and there was no more potato

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:30.610
picking, they took--

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:34.390
picked out-- we were a hundred
people there, men and women.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:37.360
So they picked out
again about 35.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.810
And I was one of them, and
my sisters, my two sisters.

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:44.110
And we were sent to Skarzysko.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:46.900
Skarzysko was worse than
any concentration camp

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.360
you can imagine.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:51.250
It was an ammunition factory.

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:55.720
And the first thing what greeted
us were people with yellow

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:57.280
faces.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:58.930
They were all yellow.

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:01.630
They worked with some
chemicals or something,

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:04.050
and they were
exposed to, I guess

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:06.050
the whatever it was there.

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:07.990
And they were all
terribly yellow.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:09.040
I was so frightened.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:11.710
I remember, it was terrible.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:17.460
And then, we were sent
to a different part.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.730
And we weren't there more
than about two, three weeks,

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:24.360
and I got very sick
with the typhus.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:27.810
We worked in a factory,
an ammunition factory.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:32.539
Now if you stayed
back, they shot you.

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:34.045
So you had to go every day.

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:41.390
They used to wake us up very
early in the morning and count.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:42.950
There was the
counting, the counting.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:44.972
The countings, to
drive us crazy.

00:08:44.972 --> 00:08:47.180
And then when we got to the
factory, we were counted.

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:49.490
And then when we came
back, we were counted,

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:51.280
constantly the counting.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:56.630
And they had those beds like
in four layers, no straw,

00:08:56.630 --> 00:08:59.240
and no nothing.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:00.860
And then people
were getting sick.

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:03.410
And I had typhus very bad.

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:05.840
My hair came out completely.

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.090
And there was no
food, naturally.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:10.400
They gave us a piece of
bread, which wasn't bread.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.940
I don't know what it
was, ersatz of some kind.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:15.700
It was one once a day.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:20.300
And once a day we got some soup
which was like dirty water.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:23.840
So when we were
sleeping, they would,

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:25.700
um, they would woke
us up one time-- no,

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:28.700
we were walking from
work through the gates,

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:30.860
and they hung a
person in the gate.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:35.940
So they hung-- we had to look
at that person for three shifts.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:43.580
And, um, this wasn't too
far from the um, latrine

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:46.570
where we had to go and we
had to run every night,

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:48.620
a few times during the night.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:50.290
And he was hanging there.

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:51.820
And somebody took off his shoes.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:52.700
He had some shoes on.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:53.962
They took off his shoes.

00:09:53.962 --> 00:09:55.420
They must-- he must
have been there

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:58.090
for about three or
four days or more.

00:09:58.090 --> 00:09:59.430
So nobody should miss him.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:00.610
So all they saw him.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:03.610
There was no food and,
uh, the beatings--

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:05.947
I still have a scar on my
leg because we were standing

00:10:05.947 --> 00:10:07.030
and counting and counting.

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:09.623
All of a sudden, they decided
to hit us over the heads.

00:10:09.623 --> 00:10:11.790
And I figured, I'm not going
to get hit for nothing.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:16.750
And I just wanted to crawl by,
so I ripped my leg with the--

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:18.270
with the wire.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:20.800
And I was bleeding, and nobody--

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.660
they didn't have anything
to put on my leg.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:25.870
And it started to
spoil very bad.

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:28.480
And, uh, so when
we worked there,

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:32.290
there were some Polish people
coming into work from the city.

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:37.570
So one woman said, uh, to
put sour milk on my leg.

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:39.790
Maybe it will go away,
because it started to swell,

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:41.800
and, uh, it was bad.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:43.370
So we hid--

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:45.760
I managed to hid my
mother's ring in my mouth

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:48.110
when we went in to the ghetto.

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:50.260
Not the ghetto, to the camps.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:52.900
So we gave her the ring and
she brought in a loaf of bread

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:57.040
and, um, a little
cup of sour milk.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:59.880
So the three of us decided
what to do, to put it on my leg

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:01.600
or eat it.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:05.770
So we decide to half on my
leg, and half to eat it.

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:08.080
Now if we wouldn't
be the three of us,

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.150
we would, we would
never survive.

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:12.730
None of us would have made it,
because we were watching out

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:13.810
for one another.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:17.260
Put us on trains to go
farther towards the west,

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:20.590
so we went to
Czestochowa, another, uh,

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:22.030
ammunition factory.

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:25.970
Somehow the Russian armies
came too fast for them,

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:30.040
and in '45 we were liberated.

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:32.980
And the feeling, it's
a terrible feeling.

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:36.460
While you in camp, you don't
know what tomorrow will bring.

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:40.990
But what made me go is I
thought my father was hiding

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:43.000
in a village with
my little brother,

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:44.300
that he will survive there.

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:46.920
And I always, I was very,
very attached to my father.

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:50.610
He was, was over 6' 4".

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:53.760
He was beautiful person,
and a beautiful human being.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:55.972
And I thought he'll
survive there.

00:11:55.972 --> 00:11:57.430
And I always thought
he'll need me.

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:00.010
I'll, I'll have to help him.

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:03.070
He'll sicks, oh, like
I am and he needs help.

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:04.840
This what made me go.

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:05.980
And in order to--

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:09.070
it's funny, I'm thinking
of that now that, um,

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:10.720
how I split myself.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:12.590
That it wasn't me there.

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:13.820
It just wasn't me.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:15.040
I was somebody else.

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:16.480
It wasn't me.

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:20.350
And, but unfortunate,
when we, the war ended,

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:22.410
we actually afraid
to leave the place.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:24.270
It was a very funny
feeling, the three of us

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:25.395
were sitting holding hands.

00:12:25.395 --> 00:12:26.740
We're afraid to go out.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:29.350
Finally, when we did
go out, we found,

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:32.320
we found something
terrible, like nothing.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:36.640
And, uh, and the natives
weren't too friendly either.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:41.380
I went back home, and uh,
to look for my father.

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:43.040
So naturally they--

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:43.930
I came to the city.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:46.060
There were already a few people.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:48.940
And they told me that my
father and my little brother

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:51.640
was turned out to the Germans.

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:54.130
Well, anyway, so
it wasn't too good.

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:56.170
We had to get out of Poland.

00:12:56.170 --> 00:13:00.790
And I went back to the land of
Germany, would you believe it?

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:02.717
I felt safer in Germany.

00:13:02.717 --> 00:13:04.300
Only because there
were the Americans,

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:05.910
I suppose, we felt safer there.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:08.560
And, uh, of course, I
came to the United States

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:13.380
and I met my husband here,
and we have three children.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:15.550
And, uh, every
time, this is when

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:18.670
I became pregnant
with my children,

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:20.590
I-- it just like I woke up.

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:22.930
I got terribly frightened.

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:27.520
And ,um, every time I thought
of that woman, Rachel.

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:30.640
Rachel Goldfarb was her name,
what happened to her baby?

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:35.440
What, uh-- she was very
close to having her baby.

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:39.580
And her face is
almost in front of me.

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:42.010
What did they do to the baby?

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:43.686
What they do to her?

00:13:43.686 --> 00:13:46.380
And here I am.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:49.350
And I did have the
children, because, uh,

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:52.770
I always said if, um, that's
what Hitler would want to, we

00:13:52.770 --> 00:13:55.050
shouldn't have any children,
or we shouldn't-- we just

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:56.400
disappear.

00:13:56.400 --> 00:14:01.000
So my children I named after my
father, my brother, my mother,

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:02.630
and my sister.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:06.320
They always question me, because
you know, the old story, "Where

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:07.680
our grandparents?

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.740
Tell me about--" They were very
curious, very bright children.

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:14.370
"Tell me about your
about our grandparents.

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:15.470
How were they like?

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:17.440
And what was your
hometown like?"

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:20.980
I have like a panorama
in front of me.

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:25.666
I could be in a room
full of people, and um,

00:14:25.666 --> 00:14:27.780
if-- there was a time
when I was, let's say,

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:29.200
in Skarzysko I didn't--

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:30.940
I didn't talk at all.

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:34.340
And, um, I just, I just
couldn't believe it.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:35.860
I just didn't talk.

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:39.730
And then I was constantly
talking after the war,

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:41.800
because then I realized
I, I was afraid.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:44.470
If I won't talk,
I'll see everything.

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:47.620
And even now, when,
um, when I vacuum,

00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:53.450
or I do some tedious
household thing, I always cry.

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:56.620
I always see-- because
I could be at that--

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:58.320
with 100 people.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:01.780
And if I sit quiet, it's
just like a panorama

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:03.640
with all that stuff
coming through my head.

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:05.027
Nobody's left.

00:15:05.027 --> 00:15:05.860
And we were always--

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:07.697
my house was always
full of people,

00:15:07.697 --> 00:15:10.030
because all this time it was
my grandmother was with us,

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:12.290
so everybody was
coming to my house.

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:17.086
And, um, so I always
think about those people.

00:15:17.086 --> 00:15:19.620
And I'm always
afraid that I'll--

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:21.670
I don't have any
pictures of them.

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:26.740
And then always wonder about
my little brother or my father,

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:29.080
because the two of
them were together.

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:30.550
You always say,
"How did they die?

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:31.660
What happened?"

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:35.080
It's something you don't forget.

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:37.870
And, and the horror
of it is, every time

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:42.490
I see the newspaper now, and,
uh, with Nazis, and how they--

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:44.170
it's just that it never ends.

00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:45.820
It's no end to it.

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:48.350
And this frightens me very much.

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:50.230
And I'm not trying to
show to the children.

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:51.355
I don't want to scare them.

00:15:51.355 --> 00:15:56.530
The-- the men who already,
you know, grown people.

00:15:56.530 --> 00:15:59.220
And uh, I'm scared.

